TEXT -392 -16 Left 2 !.options vntol=1m abstol=1m srcsteps=2 gminsteps=0\n.param tdv=1 tdt=1 fn1=50 omega_n={2*pi*fn1}\n.param sfactor=3 vfactor=sqrt(3)\n \n.op\n.step param x list 1 2 3 ; dummy steps
TEXT -168 0 Left 2 ;Impedance of transformer\nand net in one model \nin combination with a ideal\ntransformer without impedance
TEXT 8 24 Left 2 ;ideal transformer 1:1
TEXT -360 352 Left 2 ;Votage profile with l_batt and transformer taping (voltage regulation atf high voltage side)\n(Hint: The voltage level in paranthesis are the values without taping)\n \nUpper circuit: left transformer with l_=-2 taping (Translation factor changed by-2*1.5%)\n \nThe reactive incuctive power flows always from the higher to the lower voltage!\nSince the left transformer is taped by -3% (taping at the high voltage side)\nthe voltage of the lower side increase and the voltage of the upper side decrease\nSince the voltage at Pt2 is now lower as before (113kV to 111.7kV) there is more flow of\nreactive power (47MVAR) from the left voltage source (and fewer from the right side) \nAt the left transformer there is now a voltage jump from 111.7kV to 115.2kV (Pt3) and\na voltage of 114.3kV at the shunt reactor which is higher as the voltage without taping\nThe voltage profile from the right side is rather flat because the flow of inductive power is\nmuch smaller now (8.4MVAR)\n \n \nLower circuit: both transformer with L_=-2 taping and shunt reactor\n \nSince now both transformers are tapped by 3% the flow of inductive reactive power\nis now again a evenly load for the two transformers.\nSince the powerflow from the left side is now smaller the as before the voltage drop\nis also smaller and the voltage at the shunt reactor is now approx. 1.5kV higher than\nbefore. The voltage profile of the right side is now a mirror of the left side.
TEXT -360 600 Left 2 ;Exercise:\nExecute this example also with the transient toolbox\nLater see there in the example section
